    <fr:title text="Wrapping and On-Chain Verification">Wrapping and On-Chain Verification</fr:title>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    The last step of a deployed pipeline: convert a large STARK proof into something an on-chain verifier can check cheaply, usually by proving the STARK verifier inside a <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0032/" title="KZG and the Pairing-Based Line" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0032/" display-uri="dhsorens-0032" type="local">pairing-based</fr:link> system.
</html:p>
    <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The wrapping chain in a real system, layer by layer, with proof sizes at each step.</html:li>
        <html:li>Arithmetising a hash-based verifier: which parts are expensive and why.</html:li>
        <html:li>Gas cost of the final verifier, and what it constrains upstream.</html:li>
        <html:li>The trust and assumption stack the wrap introduces — a trusted setup at the end of a transparent pipeline.</html:li>
        <html:li>Whether the composed security is stated anywhere precisely enough to formalise.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
