              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Folding two <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002S/" title="R1CS" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002S/" display-uri="dhsorens-002S" type="local">R1CS</fr:link> instances into one without proving either, deferring all the SNARK work to the end. Also a case study in why this material needs formalising: a soundness bug was found in the published scheme.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Relaxed R1CS and committed relaxed R1CS: why the relaxation is needed to make folding close.</html:li>
        <html:li>The folding step and its knowledge-soundness argument.</html:li>
        <html:li>Why the recursive circuit is small — the folding verifier, not a SNARK verifier.</html:li>
        <html:li>The bug: what the flaw was, what it permitted, and which part of the argument would have caught it under <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003Z/" title="Formalising the Stack" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003Z/" display-uri="dhsorens-003Z" type="local">machine-checked</fr:link> scrutiny.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
                <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://blog.zksecurity.xyz/posts/nova-attack/" type="external">how Nova got broken</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://github.com/lurk-lab/awesome-folding" type="external">awesome-folding</fr:link>.
</html:p>
